rom that seed, in the paper's own graphical formalism, to work through its consequences, and to flag where the footnote leaves choices unresolved. Everything in the paper's main text about FDT — subjunctive dependence, the `do`-style intervention on a logical variable, the \"global perspective\" — carries over; the only change is *what* the intervention sets.\n\n## 2. Background: subjunctive dependence and the FDT idea\n\nStart with the core intuition. In Newcomb's problem, a reliable predictor fills a box based on a model of your reasoning. Your action and the box's contents are correlated, but not causally — the box was packed yesterday. Yet the correlation is not mere happenstance either: it is grounded in the fact that both your action and the predictor's prediction are computations of (or about) the *same decision function*. The paper calls this **subjunctive dependence**: \"When two physical systems are computing the same function, we will say that their behaviors 'subjunctively depend' upon that function.\"\n\nThis licenses a rough slogan: *FDT is to subjunctive dependence as CDT is to causal dependence.* A CDT agent, when evaluating an act $a$, holds fixed everything not causally downstream of the act. An FDT agent, when evaluating the output $a$ of her decision function, holds fixed everything except what subjunctively depends on that output — so the predictor's prediction, and hence the box's contents, move *with* the decision.\n\nFormally, following the source's Section 5: the agent has a world-model consisting of a distribution $P$ over variables (including logical variables, such as a variable $\\mathrm{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\underline{x})$ representing the output of the decision algorithm itself on inputs $(P, G, x)$) and a dependency graph $G$ that records how changing a logical variable would propagate. FDT then prescribes:\n\n$$\\mathrm{FDT}(P, G, x) \\coloneqq \\arg\\max_{a \\in \\mathcal A} \\mathbb E\\left( V \\mid \\mathrm{do}\\left(\\mathrm{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\underline{x}) = a\\right)\\right).$$\n\nNote two departures from CDT, $\\mathrm{CDT}(P, G, x) = \\arg\\max_a \\mathbb E(V \\mid \\mathrm{do}(\\mathrm{Act}=a), \\mathrm{Obs}=x)$:\n\n1. **Intervention point.** CDT intervenes on the physical action node $\\mathrm{Act}$. FDT intervenes on the *logical* node representing what the decision procedure outputs.\n2. **Observation handling.** CDT responds to observations by Bayesian conditioning. FDT responds by *switching which node it intervenes on*: with observation history $x$, intervene on $\\mathrm{fdt}(\\underline P,\\underline G,\\underline x)$; with $y$, intervene on $\\mathrm{fdt}(\\underline P,\\underline G,\\underline y)$. The paper puts it memorably: \"FDT says that agents should entertain the hypothesis that, if their action were different, they might have *made different observations*.\"\n\nThis second feature is what lets an FDT agent one-box in the transparent Newcomb problem while staring at a full box, and refuse the mechanical blackmailer while holding the extortion letter. She does not condition on the observation as settled news when *evaluating* hypotheticals; she evaluates the procedure that will be run *given* that observation, and asks which output of that procedure corresponds to the better world.\n\nFootnote 7 of the source discloses that \"in the authors' preferred formalization of FDT, agents actually iterate over *policies* (mappings from observations to actions) rather than actions.\" That is P-FDT, and it is what we now formalize.\n\n## 3. P-FDT: the formal definition\n\n### 3.1 Policies\n\nFix a decision problem with a countable set $\\mathcal X$ of possible observation histories and a finite action set $\\mathcal A$. A **policy** is a function $\\pi : \\mathcal X \\to \\mathcal A$. Let $\\Pi = \\mathcal A^{\\mathcal X}$ denote the policy space. The agent's actual decision procedure, given model $(P,G)$ and input $x$, computes an action $\\pi^*(x)$ where $\\pi^*$ is the policy the procedure implements; equivalently, the procedure's \"type\" is a policy, and each observation $x$ induces the \"token\" output $\\pi^*(x)$.\n\n### 3.2 The decision rule\n\nThe graphical formalism extends directly. In the agent's model, add a variable for each relevant policy-value: a variable $\\Pi^{\\mathrm{P\\text{-}FDT}}(\\underline P, \\underline G)$ representing which policy the P-FDT procedure selects given the model. (As in the source, the self-reference here is harmless and well-founded in principle — Kleene's second recursion theorem shows how an agent can contain an accurate representation of its own decision rule without circularity.) The graph $G$ records subjunctive dependence: any variable in the world whose value depends on what the agent would do *in any contingency* — any predictor's prediction of the agent's policy, any twin, any conditional mechanism — is a child of $\\Pi^{\\mathrm{P\\text{-}FDT}}$.\n\nP-FDT then prescribes:\n\n$$\\pi^\\dagger \\;=\\; \\arg\\max_{\\pi \\in \\Pi}\\; \\mathbb E\\Big( V \\;\\Big|\\; \\mathrm{do}\\big(\\Pi^{\\mathrm{P\\text{-}FDT}}(\\underline P, \\underline G) = \\pi\\big)\\Big),$$\n\nand the agent **executes** $\\pi^\\dagger(x)$ for her actually received observation $x$.\n\nRead the difference carefully. The act-level FDT of the paper's main text evaluates each output $a$ of the procedure *for the current input $x$*, with other inputs' outputs \"frozen\" as side nodes. P-FDT intervenes once, setting the *whole function* $\\pi$, and lets the downstream consequences propagate for every input simultaneously. Since the policy node's value determines what the agent would do in *every* contingency, the expectation is taken over the joint distribution of (which observation occurs, what the environment does in response to the policy). The agent then applies her actual input $x$ to the winning policy.\n\nTwo remarks on what this means operationally:\n\n- **Evaluation, not deliberation-over-moments.** P-FDT does not imagine choosing repeatedly. It imagines being the *kind of procedure* that outputs $\\pi(x)$ for each $x$, and computes expected utility over the uncertainty about which contingency actually obtains. This is the sense in which it is \"updateless\": observations are treated as inputs to a policy being evaluated, not as evidence to be conditioned on before evaluation.\n- **Relationship to act-level FDT.** In single-observation dilemmas where the current observation's output is the only thing that matters, P-FDT reduces to the act-level rule: maximizing over $\\pi \\in \\Pi$ with only $x$ relevant is equivalent to maximizing over $\\pi(x)$ with the rest of $\\pi$ fixed, which is exactly equation (4) of the source. The footnote's caveat — \"the difference only matters in multi-agent dilemmas so far as we can tell\" — is the claim that the two formulations provably coincide in the single-agent dilemmas the paper analyzes, and may diverge when *other agents* condition on how *you would have acted in other situations*.\n\n### 3.3 Why policies matter: conditioning on others' conditioning\n\nWhy should iterating over policies rather than actions matter in multi-agent settings? The key phenomenon is when another agent's behavior depends not just on what you do here, but on what you *would have done* in counterfactual situations — situations that, as it happens, do not obtain.\n\nConsider an act-level FDT agent in a dilemma with two possible observation histories, $x_1$ (which occurs) and $x_2$ (which doesn't). She intervenes on $\\mathrm{fdt}(\\underline P,\\underline G,\\underline{x_1})$, the output *given $x_1$*. But suppose her partner conditions on the agent's *disposition*: \"I'll cooperate if she's the sort who'd have paid in situation $x_2$.\" The act-level agent's intervention doesn't move the $x_2$-output node — it isn't the intervention point — so the relevant dependence is severed in her hypotheticals even though it is real in the world. P-FDT repairs this: intervening on the policy node moves *all* the contingency outputs at once, preserving exactly the dependencies that run through \"what she would do if.\"\n\nA stylized example (my own construction, in the spirit of the source's remarks; the source does not give a concrete dilemma): suppose a predictor fills box B iff you would pay \\$100 in a trivial side-game *that you will never actually face*, and the main dilemma is an ordinary Newcomb problem. An act-level FDT agent's hypotheticals in the main dilemma leave the side-game output fixed, so she treats box B's contents as independent of her main choice — losing the predictor's money. A P-FDT agent's hypotheticals vary the entire policy, so \"the kind of agent who one-boxes\" and \"the kind of agent who would pay in the side-game\" move together, and she correctly accounts for the predictor's behavior. In effect, P-FDT ensures that *all* of your conditional behavior is on the table whenever anything in the world tracks your conditional behavior.\n\n## 4. Treating counterfactuals and dependencies\n\nP-FDT inherits FDT's counterfactual machinery wholesale, so let me state it precisely, since the paper is careful here.\n\n- **What is varied:** the value of a logical variable — the output of the decision procedure (or the selected policy). The agent fully knows this is counterpossible: \"If a certain decision function outputs cooperate on a certain input, then it does so of logical necessity.\" Imagining the output differing is imagining a counterpossibility, in the sense of Cohen (1990). The source is candid that no fully satisfactory semantics for counterpossible conditionals exists, and that this is \"the main drawback of FDT relative to CDT at this time.\" It argues the difficulty is \"technical rather than philosophical,\" noting that humans reason comfortably under logical uncertainty (e.g., about the twin prime conjecture), and that the needed reasoning is narrow: \"How would this predictor's prediction of my action change if the FDT algorithm had a different output?\"\n- **What moves with it:** everything subjunctively dependent on that output — the twin's identical reasoning, the predictor's model-based prediction, your own future outputs on symmetric inputs — as mediated by the graph $G$.\n- **What is held fixed:** purely statistical correlations. The lesion in the smoking lesion problem does not compute anything about your decision, so varying your decision function's output leaves the cancer probability at its prior $p$. P-FDT agents one-box *and* smoke, dissolving the classic dilemma.\n\nNote the structural parallel the paper draws: *every* method of constructing hypotheticals generates its own notion of dominance, so dominance cannot adjudicate between theories. The paper's preferred criterion is utility achieved in \"fair\" dilemmas — those where \"the agent's beliefs are accurate and the outcome depends only on the agent's behavior in the dilemma at hand\" (with a caveat in footnote 15 that formalizing fairness across differently-punished theories remains open).\n\n## 5. Worked examples under P-FDT\n\nSince P-FDT agrees with act-level FDT on the source's single-agent dilemmas, I'll verify the reduction in one case and then give the policy machinery genuine work in two multi-contingency settings.\n\n**Newcomb's problem.** One observation history, $x$. Policies are effectively two: $\\pi_{1}$ (one-box) and $\\pi_{2}$ (two-box). The predictor's prediction is a child of the policy node with 99% accuracy. Under $\\mathrm{do}(\\Pi^{\\mathrm{P\\text{-}FDT}} = \\pi_1)$: box full with probability 0.99, expected utility \\$990,000. Under $\\pi_2$: expected utility \\$11,000. One-box, then apply $x$. Identical to the source's Fiona, as promised.\n\n**The transparent Newcomb problem.** Two observation histories: see-full and see-empty. Policies: e.g., $\\pi_{OO}$ (one-box on full, one-box on empty), $\\pi_{TO}$ (two-box on full, one-box on empty), etc. The predictor fills the box iff she predicts $\\pi(\\text{see-full}) = \\text{onebox}$. Evaluating policy $\\pi_{OO}$: with probability .99 (accurate predictor) you see a full box and one-box: \\$1,000,000; with probability .01 you see an empty box and your see-empty output (one-boxing, which gets \\$0) applies. Evaluating $\\pi_{TO}$: with probability .99 you see an *empty* box; you still two-box it for \\$1,000, plus the rare .01 case worth \\$1,001,000: about \\$11,000. The winning policy is one-box-on-full, and that is what you execute even though, as you stand there, the box visibly *is* full. The observation enters as an input to the evaluated policy, not as something conditioned away. This reproduces the source's Fiona exactly — the notation $\\mathrm{fdt}(\\underline P, \\underline G, \\textsc{full})$ in the paper's Figure for the transparent case is, in P-FDT terms, a component of the policy vector.\n\n**A coordination case (my extension).** The paper's conclusion describes voting: ten agents each pay \\$1,000 to vote, and each gets \\$1,000,000 iff all vote. Under P-FDT, each agent intervenes on her policy; since the agents reason identically (subjunctive dependence), setting your policy to \"vote\" sets theirs to \"vote,\" yielding \\$999,000 net versus −\\$1,000 or worse otherwise. The policy framing adds a genuine refinement here: each agent's *observation* includes whatever signals she receives about the others' behavior. A policy maps each signal to an action, and the intervention moves the symmetric agents' policies on *all* signals — including signals that don't occur. If, say, the vote requires unanimity and each agent might observe a defection signal, the policy evaluation automatically accounts for what everyone does in those contingencies. I flag this as my own development; the source treats the voting case only at the act level and does not analyze contingency-dependence within it.\n\n**When policy and act formulations can differ — a caution.** Here the footnote's \"so far as we can tell\" earns its hedge. Consider an iterated setting where a partner's treatment of you in round two depends on your round-one behavior *in each possible round-one situation*, only one of which actually occurs. Act-level FDT, intervening only on the actual round-one output, may fail to move the counterfactual-round-one node, and could then mis-evaluate. P-FDT handles this by construction. But this is exactly the kind of case where the source's assurance of equivalence is untested, and I would treat the reduction of P-FDT to act-level FDT as established only for single-observation and symmetric multi-agent dilemmas of the kinds analyzed in the paper. Whether there exists a fair dilemma where act-level and policy-level FDT *prescribe different actions* — as opposed to P-FDT merely evaluating more accurately — appears to be open. The footnote's phrasing (\"the difference only matters in multi-agent dilemmas\") suggests the authors believe the prescriptions coincide more often than not; I have not found a counterexample in the source's own dilemma set.\n\n## 6. Why P-FDT, philosophically\n\nThree arguments from the source carry over directly, and one is strengthened.\n\n*Reflective stability.* P-FDT agents \"act as they would have precommitted to act\" — but on the policy formulation this is nearly a tautology: the policy *is* the precommitment, selected for its global consequences. An agent facing a multi-stage dilemma never wants to switch theories mid-stream, because the value of her current stage's behavior was already priced into the policy when it was selected. This strengthens the paper's case that CDT's endorsement of self-modification (Arntzenius 2008) is a symptom of a defect: P-FDT has no temporal inconsistency to repair.\n\n*Non-negative value of information.* Because observations are inputs rather than evidence-to-be-conditioned-on, learning more never distorts the evaluation. EDT agents in the XOR blackmail problem pay blackmailers because conditioning on \"I paid\" predicts good news; P-FDT agents intervene on the policy, note that a paying policy gets blackmailed far more often than a refusing one, and refuse — even while holding the letter, even knowing the predictor erred if the letter is spurious.\n\n*The global perspective.* The paper's closing diagnosis — EDT respects too many action–environment correlations, CDT too few — becomes, at the policy level: your conditional dispositions across *all* situations are part of the causal-subjunctive fabric of the world, and anything that tracks those dispositions (predictors, partners, your future selves) will respond to them. Rationality means choosing that fabric, not just today's thread.\n\nThe open problems also carry over, sharpened: how to construct the dependency graph $G$ for subjunctive (not merely causal) relations from observation and experiment is \"perhaps the largest open problem\"; and formalizing fairness remains unfinished. P-FDT adds one more: characterizing precisely the class of dilemmas where policy-level and act-level formulations diverge, and whether any fair dilemma separates their prescriptions.\n\n## 7. Conclusion\n\nP-FDT is the policy-level formulation of functional decision theory: intervene on the variable representing which mapping from observations to actions your decision procedure implements; take the policy whose subjunctively-downstream consequences maximize expected utility; apply your actual observation as input. It reproduces all of FDT's celebrated prescriptions — one-boxing (transparent or otherwise), smoking, refusing extortion, cooperating with twins, voting with the coalition, hitchhiking out of the desert — while extending the scope of the evaluated object to cover exactly those dependencies that run through *what you would have done in situations that never arose*. The source offers the formulation in a single footnote and vouches for it only informally; the reconstruction here, and the coordination examples in Section 5, are my development of what that footnote implies. The theory's fate, like FDT's, turns on two open problems: a semantics for counterpossible reasoning, and a general method for discovering subjunctive dependency structure. But as a rule of application, P-FDT is crisp: *ask what the whole world would look like — including every contingency you might have faced — if your policy were different; then do what that policy says to do here.*\n\n**Reference:** Yudkowsky, E. & Soares, N., \"Functional Decision Theory: A New Theory of Instrumental Rationality,\" arXiv:1710.05060 (primary source; footnote 16 and footnote 7 for the policy formulation).","refusal":null,"reasoning":null}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":57808,"completion_tokens":4496,"total_tokens":62304,"cost":0.00398416,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":57792,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.00398416,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.00173616,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.002248},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":0,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}